judgments.quote:
const response = await fetch(url);
if (!response.ok) {
throw new Error(`Response status: ${response.status}`);
}
const result = await response.json();
console.log(result);
} catch (error) {
console.error(error.message);
}
}
We declare a string containing the URL and then call
fetch()
, passing the URL with no extra options.
The
fetch()
function will reject the promise on some errors, but not if the server responds with an error status like
404
: so we also check the response status and throw if it is not OK.
